and welcome to the Commission's press  room today 19th of September it's our  pleasure to have with us Commissioner  Mark oficio to discuss the state aid  decision taking just now so the  commissioner and I'll give a statement  before we then move on to our customary  Q&amp;A session commissioner well thank you  the Commission has today decided that  the non taxation of certain McDonald's  profit in Luxembourg is not illegal  state aid our case concerned two tax  rulings granted by Luxembourg to  McDonald's in 2009 these accepts from  taxation in Luxembourg all franchise  profits that McDonald's receives from  third parties operating McDonald's  outlets in Europe Ukraine and Russia in  the first ruling Luxembourg falsely  assumed that these prophets were taxable  in the u.s. they were not in the second  ruling Luxembourg then removed any  obligation on McDonald's to prove that  the revenues were taxable in the US this  means that these profits were neither  tax in Luxembourg nor in the US the  Commission investigated under EU stated  rules whether this double non taxation  was a result of Luxembourg miss applying  its national laws and the Luxembourg us  double taxation treaty in favor of  McDonald's EU in you stated rules  prevents Member States from giving  unfair advantages to only selected  companies including illegal tax benefits  however our in-depth investigation has  shown that the reason for the double non  taxation in this case is a mismatch  between Luxembourg and US tax laws and  not a special treatment by Luxembourg  there  for we concluded that Luxembourg did not  break EU state age rules let me give you  a few more details our case concerns  McDonald's Europe franchising a  luxembourg-based subsidiary of the  McDonald's Corporation this company also  has a branch in the u.s. McDonald's  Europe franchising owns the rights to  the McDonald's brands and other related  rights it licenses these rights to third  parties who operates the McDonald's fat  fast food outlets and pay franchise fees  to McDonald's Europe franchising in  February 2009 McDonald's Europe  franchising contacted the Luxembourg tax  authorities they contact them to ask for  a tax ruling confirming that profits  from its franchise rights would not be  taxable in Luxembourg McDonald's claimed  that this was because these rights were  attrition attributed to its us French it  further argued that Luxembourg us double  taxation treaty accepts from taxation in  Luxembourg any income that may be taxed  in the u.s. if of course the company has  the taxable presence there for example  through a branch in March 2009 the  Luxembourg authorities issued a first  tax ruling agreeing with McDonald's  interpretation of the double taxation  treaty at the same time they requested  that the company provide proof that the  income of the u.s. branch had been  declared and could indeed be taxed in  the US however six months later the  Luxembourg tax authorities issued a  second tax ruling that removed this  requirement to provide proof  in other words Luxembourg confirmed that  the income is except from taxation in  luck  work as well even though it's not  taxable in the u.s. branch you may  wonder how Luxembourg can rely on a  treaty meant to avoid double taxation to  endorse double non taxation we ask  ourselves the same question and this is  why we open the state aid investigation  in December 2015 the purpose of opening  such an investigation is to give Member  States and the company concerns as well  as other third parties the opportunity  to submit their views to the  Commission's preliminary concerns and  then we carefully assess them in the  short summary of our conclusions in the  case is that the double taxation treaty  between Luxembourg and the US explains  Luxembourg's tax treatment of McDonald's  Luxembourg and that they did not miss  apply the treaty in a selective manner  and on that basis they didn't break EU  state aid rules the more complicated if  this wasn't complicated enough for you  the more complicated answer starts with  a taxation concept called permanent  establishment a company has a permanent  establishment in a specific country if  this means that it carries out business  and has a taxable presence there the  Luxembourg US double taxation treaty  says that blocksberg  cannot tax the profit of companies if it  may be taxed in the US because they have  a permanent establishment there  Luxembourg can assume that the income of  this permanent establishment is being  taxed in the US however whether a  permanent establishment exists in the  u.s. is assessed differently by the US  and by the Luxembourg tax authorities  under their respective tax codes the  u.s. did  consider the u.s. branch of McDonald's  Europe franchising to be a permanent  establishment under its tax laws and so  it did not tax the profits of this US  branch  however the Luxembourg authorities  considered that the same US branch  fulfilled all the conditions necessary  to be a permanent establishment under  looks Luxembourg tax law it was  therefore exempted it therefore exempt  of this income from Luxembourg taxation  in line with a double taxation treaty  the result was double non taxation of  the income by Luxembourg and the u.s.  however it was not due to any special  treatments awarded by Luxembourg to  McDonald's which was the issue under  investigation under our state eight  rules in other words Luxembourg's tax  treatment of McDonald's is not illegal  under EU state rules as usual we will  publish the non-confidential version of  our decision as soon as we have agreed  with Luxembourg on any business secrets  that needs to be removed from it of  course the fact remains that McDonald's  did not pay any taxes in Luxembourg on  these profits and this is not how it  should be from a tax fairness point of  view and this is why I very much welcome  that the Luxembourg government is taking  legislative steps to address the issue  that arose in this case and avoid such  situations in the future among other  things Luxembourg will strengthen the  criteria under its tax code to define a  permanent establishment this proposal is  currently with the Luxembourg Parliament  once adopted the new provision will  require the taxpayer in certain  circumstance to provide a certificate of  residence in the other country  if it wants to benefit from the tax  exception in Luxembourg this would be  proof that the other country recognizes  the existence of a taxable permanent  establishment of that country this new  provision is presented to the Luxembourg  Parliament together with other measures  to transpose the e use our common and he  tax avoidance directive over the past  few years we have seen an international  determination to deal with the issue of  tax avoidance through closing loopholes  in tax laws and working with the OECD's  base erosion and profit shifting project  also within our own European community  under the responsibility of my  colleagues bodies Dombrowski and PMO's  go Vichy the Commission has pursued a  coherent strategy towards fair taxation  and greater transparency and member  states have been using this momentum to  reform their corporate taxation  framework to make it both fairer and  more efficient  finally just a short updates on the  progress made to implement the  Commission's decision requiring Member  States to recover unpaid taxes and this  is important because if not recovered  well then the companies continue to  benefit from illegal advantages in May  Luxembourg completed the recovery of  more than two hundred and eighty million  euros from Amazon of which 21 million  euros is interest I'm also happy to  confirm that Ireland has now recovered  the full illegal aid from Apple the  final amount recovered is fourteen point  three billion euros which 1.2 billion  euros is interest this money will be  held in an escrow account pending the  outcome of the ongoing appeal of the  Commission's decision before the EU  courts this means that we can proceed to  closing the infringement process  against Ireland for failure to implement  the decision these are important steps  to tackling more to Nationals tax  avoidance and to meeting our ultimate  goal of ensuring that not all most  companies but all companies big or small  pay their fair share of taxes in the  future thank you many
 

